erage income people make out of the  out of the farm and and if you if you if  you look at the consumption patterns  about thirty percent of what people  produce is used in the in the family and  rest whatever is there is used for  generating income out of it so if I see  from a farmer's perspective what happens  is let me let me take an example of a  dairy farmer because there are there are  no dairy but if I look at the example of  this farmer's cow right about seventy  percent of the money that I need to  spend on that cow goes in buying feed or  you know in feeding the animals okay now  more I spend on the agriculture input  less is my margin on the profitability  right and therefore that is fundamental  reason why the farmers adoption rate on  agriculture technologies such as seed I  mean new seeds new braider a breeder  varieties animal health you know agro  inputs are less because of course there  is no appetite because you're you're  that that margin between between how  much you are spending to produce your  your crop or livestock and how much you  are earning from that is getting lower  and lower and  that's fundamental reason why technology  adoption becomes such a huge challenge  so just wanted to map the major  constraint this is again a farmer's  perspective to what are the major  constraints in agriculture okay what you  know every single day 2000 people leave  farming in a country like India in a  predominantly agriculture country like  India 2,000 people are leaving farming  right and the main reason is we see it  from a Productivity angle we see from a  climate change angle we see from lot of  angle for a farmer there is only one  reason that there is no profitability  and I can make more money constructing  ugh somebody's house right and so so  that's that's the reason that's the  predominant reason now if we look at  from a farmer's perspective these are  the farmers key challenges in  agriculture why they think that you know  it is becoming very difficult for them  to farm if we look at from a  Productivity angle where a lot of the  government and a lot of RND focuses  these are the only two things which are  related to productivity rest are all  mostly non-farm nothing to do with  agriculture kind of kind of factors you  know market related and N and so forth  India is one of the largest investors in  agriculture in its it spent something  like 22 billion dollars on its  agricultural sector which is one of the  largest in developing world however if  we look at where that money is going  these the sizes of these these circles  represent and it is it is it is from an  analysis of of all the different  government programs and government  schemes the largest amount of money is  going in crop production now if you if  you can please remember the previous  slide and look at the farmers perception  this was a very very small part of the  challenge that they were saying the  major part of the challenges was X  attention where the weather is very  limited effort so there is a there is a  major mismatch between between the whole  whole puzzle and and therefore when we  say R&amp;D you know technologies are  extremely important but but I would  really like to see technologies not just  from a crop productivity or production  angle but I would like to see it from a  slightly wider angle to talk about what  are the various technologies that one  can use for example market access for  nutrition and so forth and I mean market  access for example you know having all  these challenges I spoke about the Bihar  farmer and their challenges despite of  all those challenges and despite of  earning only thirty dollars from  agriculture the other fact about Bihar  also is eighty-six percent of the  households in Bihar which is one of the  poorest states eighty-six percent of the  households in Bihar have access to a  cell phone now and so how do we use  these opportunities to complete the  whole picture and so that our  productivity effort also really really  you know realize in their value and and  farmers you know even if the government  effort may be very little in these  things because it is a farmers priority  you know they put in so much it is it is  interesting I was talking to you about  the the goat project that we have this  is a this is a goat project in Bihar  where a very very large number of women  farmers who are growing goats we have a  program with them where we do the whole  value chain development animal health  and everything and it was fascinating to  see these women on their own absolutely  zero credit to our foundation or our  investment or anything because we were  focusing mainly on the animal health  aspect and all that completely on their  own they started taking pictures of  their goat with their little cell phones  and uploaded it on the ebay like Indian  site which is OLX which is an ebay like  Indian site and sold their goat as far  as about 700 800 kilometers away and the  women who sold their go there there the  price that they go they got for their  for their goats selling it far from that  ebay side was was higher anywhere  between twenty to thirty seven percent  so you know looking at constraint but  looking at some of these opportunities  and how they can use it and you know  very interestingly i was i was doing a  seminar at Oxford University and must  have said something like this and  somebody challenged but how can you say  that because you know there is there are  so many states in India where you know  electricity is such a problem how can  you think that you know and I I frankly  didn't have an answer to that and I went  back and we spoke to our field people  and things like that if we look at a  place like orissa there are rural  entrepreneurs who have now developed  what happens is on a bicycle they have a  solar panel the the young fellow goes to  a village and he's in the village for  four hours everybody gets their cell  phone charged he charges some sixty  scent or something for cell phone he  goes to another village he gets and then  he comes back to this village every two  day so every two days you can get your  cell phone charged fully right amazon  for example you know there was a there  was this thing about you know can we  sell seeds through amazon and some  people thought oh my god how naive right  i mean who is going to buy seeds through  amazon there are rural entrepreneurs in  places like Jharkhand and other states  and this is happening in Africa as well  where they go to a village with their  little little laptops the people gather  around them they order things online it  could be a plastic bucket it could be  seed it could be whatever children's  clothes the entrepreneurs order it  online then they go back and after ten  days whenever they come back they  they're things with them so there is a  huge rural market opening it does not  limit itself from whether you have  internet connectivity or not you don't  have to have a computer at home to be  able to order through amazon you don't  need to have electricity to be able to  charge your cell phone so there are  endless possibilities so I think looking  beyond just the productivity and  production lens to technology  development looking at some of these  areas and and that's what I just wanted  to you know breeding for profitability  and not just productivity I think is is  an important aspect the reason I i put  these three points down this morning is  because i am cognizant of the of the  international audience i'm speaking to  right when we at the foundation were  revisiting our india investment strategy  one of the questions that we constantly  asked ourselves and Brantley my my  colleague is here from the foundation  also we kept brainstorming on this and  said what is it that our small  foundation like us do in a large system  like India which invest 22 billion  dollars what is it that we can do that  the country is already not doing right  and thence when we came up with some of  these things as to what are what are  those value adds that weekend we can  bring in when there should be focused so  breeding for profitability would be one  idea I wanted to bring breeding for  mixed farming and I have an example of  that and and what I mean when we breathe  for a crop keeping the mixed farming  system in mind where we know that you  know sixty percent of the of the  animal's farm animals are fed with crop  residue and not by feed that is bought  from from a shop breeding for dual  purpose which is which is increasing the  food value of it also but also looking  at the feed value because the feed the  animal is equally important to that crop  farmer  is this and therefore looking at it from  a form of mixed farming lens rather than  a then just a crop lens or a livestock  length oh sorry and my last point there  was on nutrition I think you know  nutrition in both ways in the in the  existing rice wheat system around the  world how do we sort of fortify that and  how do we make that more nutrition  because we know that it is not really  contributing to people's nutrition lead  how do we how do we augment and  value-add there is one area and the  second thing is also about you know  diversification something simple like an  early early harvest late sowing rice  variety for example where a farmer gets  one hole full season in between to be  able to grow legumes for example you  know so so nutrition in from from many  many angles oh so keeping all that in  mind you know the foundation has been  investing in India since 2007 and we've  had a large number of investments they  were they were interestingly like any  other investments were very production  productivity oriented investments and  we've learnt a lot from those we last  year we decided to completely change and  revamp our agricultural investment  strategy in India to respond to and to  leverage on the government spending and  so forth and what we you know rather  than looking at it from a science or  from a Productivity only lens we we see  that whatever efforts we make in India  should be able to contribute towards  three things income and not just  productivity productivity as a means to  achieve that income but productivity not  as the end and so farmers income  nutrition especially the what we call it  as food system whereas  culture and nutrition comes together and  we have a large program here with  Cornell University as well on on that  and of course keeping the women farmers  in mind  of course I mean you know given given  the large of geography we decided to  focus on a few things and learn from  that and take it to other states yeah so  at the end I wanted to say you know it  would be good to take a sort of a  holistic view and not just look at it  from an R&amp;D perspective RND to do what  is the question that we should be asking  ourselves and we could perhaps you know  ride the elephant together in Ithaca  thank you this has been a production of  Cornell University on the web at  cornell.edu


